* [daveey] A user Stop beats the core-update pause of the turn it stops

A Stop that landed while the update-pause hook ran (or right after it returned)
ended the turn as stopped, but the hook had stamped the interrupted-turn marker
`core_update_pause` and told the session to keep it, so the restarted backend's
startup sweep (and the in-place watcher) continued a turn the user had stopped.
At a step boundary the turn was even announced "Paused for a Hermes update;
continues automatically". Both ways a pause lands were affected: the boundary
pause and the mid-tool cancel.

Now the Stop wins atomically, all in the update_pause modules:
- boundary_pause_hook declines (under history_lock) when a user interrupt is
  pending on the agent; a backend exit's or lease takeover's interrupt is not one.
- session.interrupt drops the running turn's paid pause (user_stop_wins, same
  lock) before it retires the marker, so the marker retires like any stopped turn's.
- the agent loop ends the turn as interrupted, without another model call, when
  a user Stop is pending after the hook.
- the turn's end announces/keeps the pause only while it still stands
  (pause_stands); a turn whose hook paused it but which ended stopped drops it.

Unchanged: a pause with no Stop resumes; an idle Stop after the turn ended
paused keeps the paused request; a backend exit keeps the pause marker.

